TITLE: LLM-Based Intelligent Agents for Cybersecurity: A Tutorial and Survey of Automated Vulnerability Discovery
TEXT: Received 28 May 2026, accepted 22 June 2026, date of publication 26 June 2026, date of current version 9 July 2026. Digital Object Identifier 10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3707573 LLM-Based Intelligent Agents for Cybersecurity: A Tutorial and Survey of Automated Vulnerability Discovery ROBEL ADDIS, KIRAN PORTER, JAYDEN RYAN , MICAH STULL, SHENGJIE XU , (Senior Member, IEEE), PAUL WAGNER , ROBERT HONOMICHL , AND STEVEN FORSYTH College of Information Science, The University of Arizona, Tucson, AZ 85721, USA Corresponding author: Shengjie Xu (sjxu@arizona.edu) This work was supported in part by the Tides Foundation (Prime Sponsor: Google Inc.) ‘‘Supporting Communities and Small Business by Conducting Risk and Vulnerability Assessments through Student Experiential Learning Opportunities’’ under Award ID TF2405-120744, and in part by Arizona Cybersecurity Clinic at The University of Arizona. ABSTRACT This paper provides a tutorial and survey of LLM-based agents for automated vulnerability discovery and penetration testing. The rapid advancements in Large Language Models (LLMs) have opened new possibilities for their application in cybersecurity. Unlike traditional tools, LLMs can process natural language, retain context, and perform structured reasoning, enabling automation of complex security workflows. By equipping these models with memory mechanisms and tool-use interfaces, researchers have developed LLM-driven autonomous agents capable of performing specialized tasks such as web application testing, source code auditing, fuzzing, and network security assessment. Multi-agent architectures have emerged in which agents assume specialized roles (e.g., distinct penetration-test phases or exploit types). In addition to surveying existing applications, this work provides a step-by-step walkthrough of integrating agentic AI into penetration testing workflows. The walkthrough explores four phases: (I) mission scoping and prompt engineering for test definition and constraint enforcement, (II) autonomous exploration and tool selection for target interaction, (III) vulnerability hypothesis formation and verification through experiment design and feedback, and (IV) payload generation and refinement to transform validated findings into concrete exploits. The survey synthesizes 155 cited sources (2022–early 2026) using explicit inclusion/exclusion criteria, with coverage extending to reasoning-class models, the Model Context Protocol ecosystem, autonomous bug-bounty agents, and standardized agentic security benchmarks. Despite progress, challenges persist, such as model hallucination, context limitations, operating costs, and ethical concerns. Future directions include advancements in multi-agent collaboration, mechanisms for agent selfimprovement, and improved transparency into agent behavior. INDEX TERMS Large language models (LLMs), AI agents, cybersecurity, autonomous penetration testing, prompt engineering, vulnerability discovery, static application security testing (SAST), multi-agent systems, chain-of-thought (CoT), ReAct, Plan-then-Execute (PtE). I. INTRODUCTION A. CONTEXT AND MOTIVATION Cybersecurity operations today are defined by an unprecedented scale and growing complexity. Enterprise networks, The associate editor coordinating the review of this manuscript and approving it for publication was Turgay Celik . cloud infrastructures, and software supply chains evolve faster than conventional information security defensive measures can adapt. Classical approaches such as manual penetration testing, rule-based Static Application Security Testing (SAST), and procedural network scanning remain indispensable, yet they struggle to address the velocity and variety of modern threat landscapes [1], [2]. Prior 100884 2026 The Authors. This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 License. For more information, see https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ VOLUME 14, 2026 R. Addis et al.: LLM-Based Intelligent Agents for Cybersecurity work has explored automated penetration testing through other means, such as reinforcement learning for post-breach scenarios [3]. Recent advances in LLMs have introduced a new paradigm: intelligent agents capable of reasoning about context, interpreting code semantics, and orchestrating security toolchains autonomously. Research by Keltek et al. [1] on LLM-Supported SAST (LSAST) shows that coupling traditional static analyzers with LLM post-processors enables deeper semantic correlation across codebases, which is an ability unattainable in purely rule-driven systems. Likewise, Nakatani [4] and Luong et al. [2] demonstrate how multiagent penetration testing frameworks can coordinate reconnaissance and exploitation through adaptive orchestration, integrating tools such as Nmap and dnsenum with reasoning modules that interpret intermediate findings. LLM-assisted fuzzing for software security represents another emerging application [5]. These innovations mark the emergence of AI-augmented security intelligence, systems that replicate aspects of expert judgment by analyzing results, prioritizing vulnerabilities, and refining actions in real time. Frameworks like VulnBot [6] and xOffense [2] exemplify this evolution, embedding LLM reasoning within feedback-driven pipelines that accelerate discovery and reduce human oversight. What distinguishes this technological moment is not merely faster automation but a deeper cognitive integration between reasoning and action. As explored throughout this paper, LLMbased agents represent an emerging class of cybersecurity systems capable of traversing the entire reasoning loop, from prompt to payload, bridging perception, interpretation, and exploitation in a unified and adaptive process. B. PROBLEM DEFINITION AND GAP Despite rapid progress in AI-augmented cybersecurity, current research remains fragmented across specialized domains. Individual studies have focused on narrow capabilities, such as autonomous web exploitation, code auditing, or network scanning, but few have examined how these components integrate into a unified, reasoning-driven workflow. Traditional surveys on AI in cybersecurity typically emphasize model performance and dataset composition, with less emphasis on agentic process design; that is, how perception, reasoning, and action are coordinated through modular LLM architectures. This fragmentation leaves several open gaps. First, the absence of a shared reference architecture obscures how tools like VulnBot [6], RapidPen [4], AutoPentester [7], or LSAST [1] operationalize the reasoning loop from observation to execution. Second, limited interpretability and lack of benchmarking make it difficult to assess reliability across frameworks. Third, existing literature seldom explains how prompting techniques, reasoning chains (ReAct, Planthen-Execute), and structured workflows can be tuned for secure, verifiable automation. Consequently, practitioners and learners in the field face a growing need for an integrative resource that encompasses these three aspects: 1) Explaining the underlying mechanisms VOLUME 14, 2026 of LLM-driven agents, 2) Synthesizing the diverse experimental systems into a coherent taxonomy, 3) Establishing conceptual continuity between traditional and AI-driven security pipelines. This paper aims to address these gaps by offering both a tutorial foundation and a comparative survey of intelligent security agents. C. AIM AND SCOPE Recent systems have demonstrated impressive capabilities in isolated tasks such as navigating web applications, interpreting code semantics, or coordinating external security tools; however, integrated guidance that explains how these components operate together within a coherent agentic workflow remains scarce. The following paragraphs clarify the scope of this survey and the audience it serves. The scope of this survey focuses specifically on LLMpowered agents within cybersecurity, not on machine learning for cybersecurity as a whole. The intention is not to catalog every AI-based security tool. Instead, the paper examines how current agent architectures combine natural language reasoning, planning, memory mechanisms, and tool interaction to perform complex security operations with a degree of autonomy that was previously unattainable. By concentrating on these systems, the survey highlights the design patterns that shape their behavior, the operational loops that guide their decision-making, and the emerging conventions that define how they transform high-level objectives into actionable security outcomes. Beyond exploring existing approaches, this survey also serves as an educational foundation. Readers who are new to agentic AI will find a structured introduction to concepts such as chain-of-thought reasoning, ReAct (Reason + Act) style prompting, function calling, and multi-step planning. They will benefit from comparative analysis across application domains, as well as a detailed walkthrough of the complete workflow that connects prompts to payloads. Through this dual purpose, both tutorial and survey, the paper provides a cohesive view of the mechanisms, capabilities, and limitations of current LLM-based security agents. To sharpen differentiation from prior cybersecurity survey papers, this work centers on an operational lens: how LLMbased systems move from mission scoping to tool-mediated exploitation while preserving safety constraints, traceability, and verification checkpoints. The tutorial component is intentionally scoped to concepts directly needed for interpreting surveyed frameworks, while the comparative sections prioritize cross-framework synthesis and design trade-offs. D. SURVEY METHODOLOGY This survey follows a structured evidence-gathering process to improve transparency and reproducibility. The search process focused on peer-reviewed papers and high-quality preprints spanning approximately 2022 to early 2026, when modern agentic LLM security systems emerged rapidly. Query families included terms such as ‘‘LLM AI agents,’’ ‘‘Plan-and-Execute reasoning,’’ ‘‘tool use,’’ 100885 R. Addis et al.: LLM-Based Intelligent Agents for Cybersecurity ‘‘function calling,’’ ‘‘penetration testing,’’ ‘‘vulnerability scanning,’’ ‘‘API penetration testing,’’ and ‘‘self-improving agents.’’ Inclusion criteria required direct relevance to LLM-based agent architectures, workflows, or evaluations for vulnerability discovery, penetration testing, source-code security analysis, or network-security operations. Exclusion criteria removed studies centered only on non-agentic ML classifiers, purely theoretical discussions without architectural or operational detail, duplicate reports of the same system without substantial updates, and works lacking enough methodological detail to support reliable comparison. Screening proceeded in three stages: (1) title/abstract filtering for domain fit, (2) full-text review for architectural and evaluation detail, and (3) subsection assignment by primary contribution (web/API testing, SAST/code auditing, network/infrastructure, or cross-cutting workflow/challenges). Because reported metrics are heterogeneous across papers, comparative tables retain source-reported values and explicitly mark missing fields as NR (Not Reported) instead of imputing estimates. The resulting corpus primarily informed three application subsections in Section III, with additional foundational and safety/governance references used in Sections II, IV, and V. This process provides a reproducible, auditable survey backbone while preserving flexibility for a fast-evolving literature. Following a PRISMA-style accounting (adapted for a literature survey), the search initially identified over 200 candidate records (based on deduplicated results across academic databases: IEEE Xplore, ACM Digital Library, Scopus, Google Scholar, and curated preprint sources including arXiv cs.CR, cs.AI, cs.SE). Title/abstract screening retained approximately 160 records; full-text review retained 155 unique sources that are cited in this manuscript (the count grew modestly from the initial submission due to 2025–2026 developments added during revision). The retained references distribute approximately as follows: about 18–22 inform the LLM and agentic-foundation tutorial in Section II, about 28–34 inform the three application subsections in Section III (web/API testing, SAST and code auditing, network/infrastructure scanning), about 16–20 underpin the prompt-to-payload workflow in Section IV, and about 30– 40 ground the safety, governance, dual-use, and futuredirection discussion in Section V. Counts are approximate because a substantial subset of references is cited in more than one section (e.g., MAPTA, AutoPT, HPTSA, IRIS, VulnBot, xOffense, and PentestGPT recur across the survey, workflow, and challenges sections). This survey’s primary focus is offensive and evaluative agents for vulnerability discovery. Adjacent applications that share the same agentic substrate (malware reverse engineering, supply-chain package vetting as briefly discussed in Section III-B, defensive SOC automation, and LLM-based digital forensics) are acknowledged as promising extensions but are not surveyed comprehensively. 100886 E. STRUCTURE OVERVIEW After this introduction, Section II offers a tutorial-style explanation of the essential components that enable LLMbased agents to operate. It begins with the Large Language Model itself, including its handling of context, its instructionfollowing behavior, and its prompting strategies. The section then describes the agentic loop that links perception, reasoning, and action. It concludes by examining memory systems and tool-use interfaces, which together allow an agent to interact effectively with the external world. Section III transitions from foundational concepts to practical applications by surveying how LLM agents are currently used across major security domains. These domains include web application and Application Programming Interface (API) penetration testing, automated source code auditing, and network and infrastructure scanning. Each subsection highlights the common techniques, reasoning strategies, and operational patterns observed across existing frameworks. The section ends with a comparative analysis that distills recurring design choices, limitations, and differences across the approaches reviewed. Section IV provides a practical walkthrough of the full agent-driven security workflow, from mission scoping through payload refinement, demonstrating how high-level instructions become structured actions within an autonomous penetration testing lifecycle. Section V addresses the open challenges involved in deploying or developing LLM-based agents. It discusses technical issues such as hallucination, context length constraints, and cost considerations. It then examines broader safety and ethical concerns, including containment, dual-use risk, and the potential misuse of autonomous capabilities. The section concludes by outlining future research directions, including advances in multi-agent collaboration and mechanisms that promote transparency and self-improvement. Finally, the paper concludes by summarizing the central themes and emphasizing the significance of LLM-driven agents as a transformative step in automated cybersecurity operations. II. ANATOMY OF AN LLM-BASED SECURITY AGENT A. THE CORE ENGINE: THE LARGE LANGUAGE MODEL 1) CONTEXT WINDOWS A central concept in understanding how LLMs process information is the context window, which refers to the amount of text that a model can process at once during inference. In essence, the context window functions as the model’s working memory, enabling it to maintain coherence through sequential tokens and preserve dependencies in language generation [8]. Models such as GPT-4, Claude 3, and Llama 3 vary in their context window capabilities, with some extending to millions of tokens, as shown in Table 1. These extended windows allow LLMs to process longer sequences of text, which, in the context of this paper, are particularly useful for tasks in vulnerability discovery that involve analyzing extensive documentation, logs, or code. VOLUME 14, 2026 R. Addis et al.: LLM-Based Intelligent Agents for Cybersecurity TABLE 1. Representative LLM context window specifications (approximate vendor-reported values, 2024–2026 reasoning-class models; not benchmark results). isolate salient information, thereby reducing sequence length without fully sacrificing context [22]. More recent architectural innovations such as SelfExtend introduce multi-level attention mechanisms that enable models to map unseen token positions into familiar ranges, effectively extending their usable context without additional fine-tuning [23]. Context windows represent both strengths and limitations for LLMs. They enable models to sustain reasoning over extended inputs, yet their fixed nature and susceptibility to performance decline with long text sequences require ongoing innovation. For LLM-based security agents, understanding and optimizing the context window is crucial, as the complexity of real-world vulnerability data often exceeds the reliable operating range of existing models. The 2024–2026 reasoning-model class (e.g., OpenAI o3/o4-mini, DeepSeek-R1, Claude 3.7/4 Sonnet, Claude 4.5/4.6 Opus, Gemini 2.5/3 Pro, Llama 4) introduces a qualitative shift relevant to security agents: these systems allocate substantial test-time compute to internal deliberation chains before responding, going beyond reliance on parameter scale alone [9], [10], [11], [12], [13], [14], [15], [16]. For penetration-testing agents, this trades latency and per-call cost for stronger multi-step planning and selfverification, which can directly reduce hallucinated exploit hypotheses (Section V-A1) and improve plan quality in longhorizon agent loops. Several surveyed frameworks (e.g., AutoPT, HPTSA, MAPTA) report sensitivity to underlying model capability [17], [18], [19]; with reasoning models, planner roles benefit disproportionately while bulk toolcalling executors can often remain on cheaper, faster models. The cost discussion in Section V-A3 is updated to reflect this asymmetric deployment pattern. However, empirical research shows that longer context does not always lead to better performance. Liu et al. [20] found that LLMs often exhibit a ‘‘lost in the middle’’ effect, where information located midway through long sequences is recalled less effectively than information at the beginning or end. Similarly, Levy et al. [21] demonstrated that model accuracy tends to plateau beyond a certain input length, indicating diminishing returns. These findings highlight that while extended context windows expand potential applications, they also introduce challenges such as performance degradation, higher computational costs, and Out-of-Distribution (OOD) effects related to positional encoding. To address these problems, several strategies have been proposed. Summarization and truncation approaches help 2) PROMPTING TECHNIQUES Prompting techniques are the practical means by which LLMs are ‘‘programmed’’ and directed toward specific tasks. In agentic systems, prompts define the procedures to follow, goals to pursue, and constraints to respect. Current publications lay out a toolkit that starts with basic zero-shot and few-shot prompts, where you either provide only the instruction or add a couple of labeled examples to shape the pattern the model should imitate [24]. Moreover, Chain-ofThought (CoT) prompts request the model to demonstrate the steps, which increases multi-step reasoning, and selfconsistency considers several reasoning paths and picks a majority answer to stabilize results [25], [26]. Another straightforward yet powerful method is role prompting whereby the model is informed to behave as an expert in a certain domain so that it forms its response in that format or tone [27]. For tasks that mix thinking and doing, prompts can incorporate reasoning with actions, like ReAct, which has the model explain what it is doing while it calls tools or looks things up. This reduces error cascades in tasks like question answering and web interactions [28]. Retrieval-augmented prompting inserts retrieved passages into the prompt so the model grounds its answers in those sources, improving open-domain question answering (QA) and reducing overall hallucinations [29]. For math, code, and structured problems, scratchpads and ‘‘program of thoughts’’ prompts help the writing of intermediate calculations or short programs before producing a final answer, thus enabling the model to self-verify [30], [31]. Verification-oriented prompts like Chain-of-Verification prompt the model to plan checks, answer them, and revise its output, which further reduces hallucinations. This is distinct from CoT, which produces step-by-step reasoning [32]. Finally, effective prompting also implies being cautious about the things you include. Controlled studies reveal that loading a large amount of irrelevant text or giving pathological instructions can confuse the reasoning process [33]. Table 2 summarizes these prompting techniques, their patterns, appropriate use cases, and associated trade-offs. VOLUME 14, 2026 100887 R. Addis et al.: LLM-Based Intelligent Agents for Cybersecurity TABLE 2. Prompting techniques for LLM Agents: Patterns, Uses, and Trade-offs (conceptual summary of surveyed techniques, not empirical benchmark results). 3) IMPORTANCE OF INSTRUCTION-FOLLOWING CAPABILITIES One of the most appealing aspects of LLM-based AI agents is their assumed ability to follow instructions in accordance with the human-defined end goal. The ability of LLMs to follow instructions provided by human users is important for practicality and safety. LLM-based agents can be more helpful in real-world tasks if their instruction-following capacity is satisfactory [34]. Beyond this practical importance, problems can also arise from inadequate instructionfollowing. Research has shown that language models and thus the AI agents they empower can produce uncertain outputs that display bias or misinformation while also posing security and privacy risks involving malicious use or data leakage [35]. Different methods have been shown to better enable LLMs to follow instructions. Instruction-tuning is a method that provides LLMs with datasets that provide direction on determining instruction-output pairs that are desirable. These datasets can be divided into two categories: humanannotated datasets and LLM-synthetic datasets. Humanannotated datasets are defined by explicit human curation, while LLM-synthetic datasets are ones curated by other LLMs [34]. Another approach to achieve greater instructionfollowing capabilities is Transfer Learning which involves providing task-specific data to the model to enhance its performance related to specialized tasks. Finally, Alignmenttuning is a method that updates models by identifying undesirable responses and incorporating human feedback to reduce recurrence [35], [36]. Recent work shows that multiple factors can affect the instruction-following capability of LLMs like task familiarity, task difficulty, and prompt phrasing with the latter having a more adverse effect on the LLMs’ ability to successfully follow given instructions. This has shown that Prompt Engineering (PE) can help increase the ability of 100888 VOLUME 14, 2026 R. Addis et al.: LLM-Based Intelligent Agents for Cybersecurity an LLM to follow provided instructions [37]. One possible advantage of using PE to improve instruction-following is that it does not require manipulation of the model’s internal components, rather it targets the clarification of the input provided to the model [38]. The importance of instruction-following in LLMs presents many different challenges going forward as their use and integration into technologies like agentic AI systems becomes more predominant. Instruction-following improvement may come at a cost. Alignment-tuning helps LLMs follow instructions more safely, but this refinement comes with a decrease in model performance [34]. Furthermore, as research strives to enhance instruction-following in LLMs, such advancements may enable exploitation that produces harmful outputs leading to negative impacts [35]. B. THE AGENTIC LOOP: FROM THOUGHT TO ACTION 1) OBSERVE–THINK–ACT CYCLE The Observe–Think–Act (OTA) cycle is the foundational loop of intelligent agents. At its core, it describes how an agent perceives its environment (observe), reasons over the information (think), and carries out a response (act). This structure, long established in robotics and classical AI, ensures that agents continuously update their internal state based on new inputs before deciding on the next step [39], [40]. Sapkota et al. [41] argue that OTA underpins both traditional AI agents, which rely on explicit sensing and planning modules, and emerging agentic AI systems, where large language models interpret textual prompts as observations, generate reasoning chains, and act through output or external tools in more open-ended domains. Modern LLM-based agents such as GPT-4 and Claude 3 exemplify an OTA cycle. For instance, in Toolformer [42] the model examines an internal prompt (observation), plans whether to invoke an API (reasoning), and executes or refrains (action). In cybersecurity, an AI agent observes suspicious network logs, reasons by correlating them with known attack patterns, and acts by alerting analysts or automatically blocking traffic as demonstrated by VulnBot [6] and VulAgent [43]. Zhang et al. [44] note that traditional frameworks treat reasoning and acting as separate steps, with external prompts determining when to switch modes. Their proposed agent models internalize this loop by unifying CoT (reasoning) with Chain-of-Action (execution), effectively transforming the cycle into Thought–Action–Observation (TAO). This shift moves the agent from reactive to autonomous; note that TAO reorders the classical Observe–Think–Act (OTA) loop above for ReAct-style agents that interleave reasoning with tool use. Table 3 illustrates this cycle in a cybersecurity incident scenario, showing how an agent iteratively moves between thought (reasoning), action (execution), and observation (feedback). Each cycle shows the agent evaluating context, performing an action, and observing the outcome, which informs subsequent reasoning. VOLUME 14, 2026 2) PROMINENT REASONING FRAMEWORKS a: CHAIN-OF-THOUGHT LLMs have immense capabilities in language understanding, but their ability to perform complex, multi-step reasoning remained limited until CoT prompting was introduced, a technique designed to elicit reasoning in LLMs by having them generate a sequence of intermediate steps that lead to a final answer [25]. CoT shows how reasoning itself can be demonstrated in context, guiding models to produce logically structured solutions through the few-shot and zero-shot learning frameworks [45]. Following its introduction, research that focused on deconstructing the mechanics behind CoT’s success revealed that the critical factor is not necessarily the factual accuracy of each step, but rather the logical structure and sequential coherence of the demonstrated reasoning process itself [46]. The Counterfactual Chain-of-Thought (CCoT) approach leverages this principle to explain model decisions by generating alternative, hypothetical reasoning paths [47]. CoT prompting has evolved from a simple technique to boost performance into a fundamental concept for understanding and explaining LLM behavior. Below is an example of the Thought–Conclusion structure in a security context: Prompt: ‘‘You are a security analysis assistant. Explain your reasoning step by step. Identify whether the login pattern is suspicious.’’ Model response with CoT: • Thought 1: The logs show 19 failed login attempts from one IP in 12 seconds. • Thought 2: The user account targeted is ‘‘admin’’, which is a high-privilege account. • Thought 3: The geolocation of the IP has never appeared in this system before. • Conclusion: This login pattern is highly suspicious and may indicate credential brute forcing. b: ReAcT (REASON + ACT) ReAct is a prompt-based framework that lets a language model mix its reasoning with concrete tool-use, so the agent can reason about what to do, take an action, read the result, then refine its plan in short cycles. Yao et al. [28] formalize this by expanding the agent’s action space to include both environment actions and language thoughts and then alternating thought-action-observation steps during a trajectory. ReAct addresses common failure nodes of reasoning-only prompting. On HotpotQA and FEVER, ReAct mitigates hallucination and error by consulting an external Wikipedia API mid-reasoning, producing decision traces that humans can inspect [28]. The broader tool-learning literature frames this process as four linked stages: task planning, tool selection, tool calling, and response generation [48]. Integrating tools in this way improves robustness to input behavior and helps the model adapt to new environments, while also increasing user trust through traceable, evidence-based steps. 100889 R. Addis et al.: LLM-Based Intelligent Agents for Cybersecurity TABLE 3. Illustrative Thought–Action–Observation cycle in a cybersecurity incident scenario (pedagogical example, not empirical benchmark data). For example, when an agent needs to check for open ports, it would follow this ReAct loop: • Thought: I need to check for open ports. I should use the Nmap tool. • Action: nmap 203.0.113.17 • Observation: Host is up. Open ports: 22, 80. • Thought: I want to know what services are on those ports. • Action: nmap -sV -p 22,80 203.0.113.17 • Observation: 22/ssh OpenSSH, 80/http nginx • Thought: I have enough for the initial note. The target has SSH and a web server. I will record this and plan basic web checks next. This example mirrors the ReAct loop: plan in language, select a tool, act, read the result, then update the plan [28]. c: PLAN-THEN-EXECUTE (PtE) The Plan-then-Execute reasoning framework focuses on the distinction between the planning operation performed by an LLM and the subsequent execution of tasks [49]. A simple form of PtE improves upon few-shot CoT and zero-shot CoT techniques where the model is explicitly prompted to break the provided problem down into subtasks (the plan) and then carry out each part of its devised plan (the execution). This form, known as Plan-and-Solve (PS) prompting, becomes PS+ when additional task-specific reminders and constraints are injected into the prompt; in other words, the added detail is the distinguishing feature of PS+ relative to baseline PS [50]. AI agents can utilize a PtE-based architecture where a reasoning-intensive LLM acts as the Planner that divides the user-defined goal into subtasks, and an Executor that carries out each subtask in pursuit of the larger objective [49]. The following is an example of using PS+ prompting to guide an agent to utilize PtE to solve an information security focused problem: Problem prompt: ‘‘I have a mail server I want to secure. I am using SMTP and IMAP for email services and must be able to use SSH to administer the server from a specific private IP address only. The only services that should be running are: SSH (TCP port/22), SMTP (TCP port/25), SMTP Submission (TCP port/587), IMAP (over TLS) (TCP port/993). I only have access to basic Linux networking tools 100890 (nmap, netstat, iptables, tcpdump). What actions should be taken to achieve this posture?’’ Plan-then-Execute Elicitation prompt: ‘‘Let’s first understand the problem, extract the relevant services and corresponding ports and devise a plan to achieve the desired security posture. Then let’s carry out the plan step-by-step, ensure that initial steps (like scanning) are generated first before moving on. For each step (1) list the commands that should be entered, (2) the expected results, (3) and failover considerations.’’ The advantage afforded by the reasoning-intensive Planner comes with the trade-off of a longer ‘‘Time-to-First-Action’’ due to the increased upfront computation time. Planning is also vulnerable to hallucination which can result in illogical planning or failure to adhere to the planned tasks [51]. Several PtE-specific frameworks exist including LangChain, LangGraph, CrewAI, and Microsoft’s AutoGen. HuggingGPT is an LLM-based agent that integrates PtE by using ChatGPT to break down user requests into smaller tasks and then selects specialized models to execute them [52]. Figure 1 illustrates the architectural interaction between the Planner and Executor agents in a typical PtE system. Step 1 represents the user-provided task or overall goal given to the agent. Step 2 represents the Planner agent breaking the overall goal into smaller subtasks to be completed downstream by the Executor. Step 3 represents the Executor agent observing the subtasks provided by the Planner to carry out in order to achieve the overall goal. Step 4 represents the Executor executing subtask(s). Step 5 represents the Executor providing updates to the Planner based on what subtasks it has completed, allowing the Planner to update its plan accordingly. C. ESSENTIAL COMPONENTS 1) MEMORY Memory is an essential part of what makes AI agents stay consistent and useful across different tasks, because it provides them with the ability to maintain context. For agents powered by LLMs, memory generally comes in two forms: short-term memory (context windows) and long-term memory (vector databases). A critical component of these models is their ability to maintain context, but they still VOLUME 14, 2026 R. Addis et al.: LLM-Based Intelligent Agents for Cybersecurity FIGURE 1. Conceptual Plan-then-Execute architectural diagram (illustrative, synthesized from surveyed literature) showing the interaction between Planner and Executor agents. face challenges. These include being expensive to operate, struggling with complex decision making, and having trouble staying coherent during long conversations [53]. Short-term memory is like the model’s temporary workspace, corresponding to the context window described in Section II-A1. It includes everything the model can currently see or consider and is limited by how much text it can handle at once; earlier parts of the interaction are forgotten once the sequence grows too long. Xiong et al. [54] show that this kind of short-term memory works well for quick reasoning, but the agent may lose track of the bigger picture during longer interactions unless there is a mechanism to retain older information. Long-term memory lets the agent remember things beyond its short-term limits. This usually means saving valuable information in an external system, often called the vector database. Past interactions are converted into vector embeddings that represent meaning and are stored for later retrieval, so the agent can search and reuse relevant information without keeping raw text in context. Sun and Zeng [55] found that using a structured memory system helps the agent retrieve relevant information efficiently. Similarly, Qian et al. [56] introduced MemoRAG, a method that combines this type of long-term memory retrieval with the model’s short-term reasoning, giving the agent both immediate coherence and the ability to recall useful information from the past. Inspired by human cognition, researchers classify long-term memory in LLMs into episodic memory (events), semantic memory (facts), and procedural memory (skills) [57]. Work on long-term memory in LLMs also applies decay and reinforcement strategies to prioritize frequently accessed information and manage growth over time [53]. 2) TOOL USE ANDFUNCTION CALLING LLMs move from text to action when they learn to produce structured function calls and when they are prompted with patterns that show when to use a tool, which tool to choose, and how to pass arguments. Two complementary strategies enable this behavior. Training-based methods teach the model to emit a consistent, machine-readable call format and to master skills such as function selection, argument binding, and multi-step chaining. Prompting-based methods keep the base model fixed and use short, program-style examples; a runtime executes the call and inserts the result back into the context [42], [58], [59], [60]. In both cases, a unified function schema and a clear list of available tools guide the model, so a request like ‘‘scan this host’’ or ‘‘run this code’’ becomes a concrete call such as nmap_scan(ip_address) or run_code(python_script). For security agents, schema validation and verification (e.g., as in ToolACE) help ensure calls are well formed and that risky tools are gated when they affect real systems [60], [61]. Building on these foundations, Toolformer demonstrates that an LLM can teach itself tool timing and formatting by inserting calls into ordinary text only when they help prediction [42]. Scale and diversity matter for generalization and robustness. ToolBench constructs large datasets of real APIs, generates diverse instructions that require tool use, and annotates solution paths as chains of calls [58], [62]. ToolACE proposes adaptive task difficulty and dual-layer verification that catch name mismatches, missing required parameters, and hallucinated values [61]. Building on this foundation, function-calling LLMs extend agent capabilities well beyond Natural Language Processing tasks [63]. Several training- and prompting-based approaches have been proposed to instruct LLMs in tool use. Direct tuning of the model is one option [63]; a second tuningbased approach uses a model such as ChatGPT as a teacher to generate instruction-following data linked to tool use, as exemplified by GPT4Tools [64]. RestGPT targets RESTful APIs through a modular framework that facilitates API calling [65], and EASYTOOL addresses inconsistent or incomplete tool documentation [66]. MultiTool-CoT, in contrast, uses a concentrated prompting approach that bundles tool inventories and few-shot examples [67]. Because toolequipped LLMs can produce real-world effects, erroneous tool returns can cascade into compounding errors; humans therefore remain responsible for outcomes, and such agents are best deployed as suggestion mechanisms under human oversight [63]. In late 2024 and 2025, two open standards have begun to consolidate this fragmented landscape into reusable interfaces. The Model Context Protocol (MCP), released by Anthropic in November 2024, provides a vendor-neutral protocol that lets agents discover and invoke tools, resources, and prompts exposed by external ‘‘MCP servers,’’ similar in spirit to the Language Server Protocol but for agent-tool interaction [68]. MCP servers for filesystems, browsers, databases, and security tooling reduce per-framework integration cost and create a common substrate for sandboxing and audit. The Agent2Agent (A2A) protocol, introduced by Google VOLUME 14, 2026 100891 R. Addis et al.: LLM-Based Intelligent Agents for Cybersecurity in 2025 and adopted by multiple vendors, complements MCP by standardizing inter-agent collaboration: capability discovery, task negotiation, and structured message exchange between agents that may run on different stacks [69]. For LLM-based security agents, MCP and A2A directly affect tool gating, least-privilege policy enforcement, and multiagent coordination patterns examined later in Sections V-B and V-B4. However, rapid MCP adoption (97 million monthly SDK downloads reported by early 2026) has outpaced security tooling [70]: the January 2026 ‘‘Clinejection’’ supplychain incident compromised approximately 4,000 developer machines through a composed attack chain in which a malicious GitHub issue title induced a code assistant to run npm install on a poisoned package, exploiting trust boundaries between MCP-connected tools rather than compromise of a single server [70]. Public surveys from the same period reported that 41% of MCP registry servers accepted unauthenticated connections and that roughly 12% of audited agent skills exhibited malicious behavior [70]. In response, the MCP-38 threat taxonomy classifies 38 distinct attack categories specific to MCP ecosystems [71], the Open Web Application Security Project (OWASP) Agentic AI Top 10 (2026 edition) formalizes agent-specific risk categories [72], and Microsoft’s MCP control-plane guidance for securing agent tool execution [73]. These developments underscore that the governance concerns in Section V-B6 are not hypothetical but already operational. III. A SURVEY OF LLM AGENTS IN ACTION: APPLICATIONS AND TECHNIQUES For convenience, Table 4 provides a brief context box for the principal LLM-based security frameworks discussed throughout this section. Acronyms and tool names are expanded on first use in subsequent prose. A. WEB APPLICATION AND API PENETRATION TESTING Penetration testing is an information security practice in which practitioners conduct sanctioned attacks against applications, networks, or systems in order to subvert the implemented security features [79]. By proactively identifying and managing risks before they are exploited, effective penetration testing can protect organizations from adverse financial and reputational consequences [80]. 1) BACKGROUND OF WEB PenTest LLM agents have evolved from proof of concept into endto-end web and API penetration tests that find and validate flaws. Recent systems target classic web flaws such as SQL injection, cross-site scripting, and broken access control while operating inside safety-scoped sandboxes. In controlled evaluations modeled on the Natas wargame, teams report challenges that explicitly include Cross-Site Scripting (XSS), SQL injection, directory traversal, authentication quizzes, and cookie analysis [76]. Multi-agent frameworks extend this 100892 TABLE 4. Framework Glossary: One-line Context for Surveyed LLM-based Security Systems (reference summary, not an evaluation). idea to the open web by splitting work across specialized actors such as navigator, recon, and exploitation [19]. 2) RECENT ADVANCES OF AI AGENTS FOR WEB PenTest Literature on agent capability is converging on three patterns. First, containerized testbeds keep agents safe while giving them realistic tools. HackSynth wraps an agent in Docker with strict firewall rules and a reproducible target set [76]. Second, multi-agent plans improve coverage. Multi-Agent Penetration Testing AI (MAPTA), a multi-agent framework whose web-focused agents pass intermediate artifacts such as Uniform Resource Locators (URLs), response bodies, and suspected payloads, extends agentic coverage across the open web [19]. Third, purpose-built systems such as PentestAgent define a scaffold of reconnaissance, enumeration, exploitation, and reporting [75]. A fourth pattern has emerged rapidly in 2024–2025: computer-use and browser-use agent substrates. Anthropic’s Computer Use API (October 2024) gives Claude direct keyboard, mouse, and screenshot-level control of a virtual desktop [81]; OpenAI’s Operator (January 2025) provides a browser-resident agent capable of navigating, filling forms, and chaining tool calls in a sandboxed environment [82]; and open-source projects such as Browser-Use [83] and Skyvern [84] expose programmable web-browsing agents that combine LLM reasoning with vision-grounded DOM/UI interaction. For LLM-based web and API penetration testing, these substrates substantially lower the engineering cost of (i) realistic interaction with single-page applications VOLUME 14, 2026 R. Addis et al.: LLM-Based Intelligent Agents for Cybersecurity FIGURE 2. Conceptual four-phase workflow (illustrative, synthesized from surveyed frameworks) for LLM-based web and API penetration testing: planning, discovery, attack, and reporting. and authentication flows, (ii) replay and auditing through screenshot/event traces, and (iii) reproducible sandboxing of exploration sessions. They also expand the agent-specific attack surface (Section V-B): screenshots and rendered page content become first-class observations, which makes indirect prompt injection through page text or alt-text a concrete threat that web-pentest agents must explicitly model. More broadly, natively multimodal models such as Gemini 3 Pro [15] open the door to vision-grounded vulnerability detection (for instance, identifying visual phishing artifacts, recognizing misrendered UI states indicative of DOM manipulation, or interpreting CAPTCHA and 2FA flows), extending the agent’s perceptual repertoire beyond text-only DOM parsing. Figure 2 depicts the four-phase workflow commonly adopted by LLM-based web and API penetration testing systems. SQL injection remains a central target. SqliGPT validates a workflow with three modules: a crawler that collects request–response pairs, a replayer that can mutate inputs and resend them, and a scanner that evaluates databaseerror signatures [77]. The prompt-to-SQL (P2SQL) literature warns that models embedded inside web apps may actually generate the exploit. A recent study documents prompt-toSQL injection attacks across popular frameworks [85]. 3) PHASES FOR LLM-BASED WEB AND API PENETRATION TESTING The four-phase workflow referenced in Figure 2 can be summarized as follows. Phase 1 (Planning): Planning sets scope, runtime, and safety limits and fixes the agent loop and HTTP tooling. Containerized testbeds with strict firewalling and complete logging provide a controlled environment for end-to-end runs and align with multi-agent designs that coordinate roles for coverage and reproducibility [19], [75], [76]. Phase 2 (Discovery): Agents follow a cycle of fetch, parse, decide, and act: from a seed URL they issue requests, follow links or discover endpoints, and maintain a frontier of targets. Effective steps are often short HTTP calls plus simple VOLUME 14, 2026 text processing; headless runtimes (e.g., for JavaScriptheavy pages) serve when static parsing is insufficient [76]. In multi-agent settings, a navigator proposes next hops and a reasoning agent scores their utility before the executor makes the request, which helps on multi-step tasks such as staged XSS [19]. SqliGPT’s crawler supports discovery by collecting request–response pairs and preserving cookies, headers, and encodings for later replay [77]. Phase 3 (Attack): Form filling and payload injection require accurate request extraction and replay. A common pattern is to build or reuse a minimal representation of the original request, preserve method and headers, and vary only parameters of interest; agents often try URL-encoded and JSON bodies, propagate anti-CSRF tokens, and escalate from benign probes to riskier payloads based on server feedback [75], [76], [77]. Session management is critical: persistent cookies and token renewal must be handled explicitly; API-focused work (e.g., APIRL (deep reinforcement learning for REST API fuzzing), a learning-based REST API fuzzing framework) integrates auth refresh, throttling, and retry into the agent’s policy [19], [86]. For SQL injection, agents combine payload mutation with server-side error or result-set validation; for XSS, they search for reflection points then confirm execution via markers in returned HTML or stored XSS; for insecure direct object reference (IDOR) and related broken access control, they swap identifiers in URLs or bodies and compare responses [19], [75], [77]. Phase 4 (Reporting): Reporting aggregates evidence, actions, and outcomes into reproducible artifacts. The literature points to a practical baseline: a safe testbed, robust HTTP tooling, cookie and token persistence, a disciplined replay loop for forms, and guards that can veto risky outputs [75]. 4) REPRESENTATIVE FRAMEWORKS a: AutoPT (FSM-BASED) AutoPT is a framework inspired by the Finite State Machine (FSM) model. This design helps address memory-related problems, agents getting stuck on tasks, and inference limits 100893 R. Addis et al.: LLM-Based Intelligent Agents for Cybersecurity that can lead to overall failure, in contrast to attack-treestyle designs such as PentestGPT’s penetration testing task tree (PTT) [17], [74]. The FSM-inspired Penetration Testing State Machine (PSM) has two core states: the Agent state and the Rule state [17]. The Agent state comprises LLM agents and performs via different substates covering scanning, reconnaissance, and exploitation; it consumes a prompt with operational description, role-playing instructions, tool information, and a thought-action response format, then iterates tool use until a human-defined limit or an exit decision. The Rule state interprets the Agent’s output via predefined rules, selects vulnerabilities for exploitation by severity and ease, and checks success against expected outcomes. Parsing functions between states extract only the information relevant for tool calling or vulnerability categorization. AutoPT’s toolset is organized into Terminal (a Kali Linux Docker container with root access), Playwright (headless browser interaction via a LangChain-based library), and Search (e.g., first-page Google results for reconnaissance) [17]. Compared to the ReAct framework, AutoPT successfully completed double the number of simple tasks and nearly 10 times more complex ones; its modularization distributes information across Agent states and helps reduce the impact of hallucinations and simple-task failures. AutoPT shows economic promise, averaging $0.02423 per website test (GPT-4o mini model usage). The framework was tested against a practical target (HackTheBox UnderPass machine [87]) and successfully compromised the system 80.6% faster than the fastest human [17]. Figure 3 illustrates the AutoPT FSM workflow. Notably, AutoPT demonstrates strong results with opensource models such as DeepSeek and Qwen, which performed close to or even surpassed the commercial GPT-4o model. This success with open-source models indicates practicality regarding local model deployments that could enable offline testing and address privacy concerns. Even with the improvements provided by AutoPT’s architecture, hallucinations during longer and more complex reasoning chains can degrade outcomes. Model security policies can also be an encumbrance, sometimes refusing to carry out penetration testing tasks even when provided role-playing prompts [17]. b: HPTSA (HIERARCHICAL PLANNING AND TASK-SPECIFIC AGENTS) HPTSA stands for ‘‘Hierarchical Planning with Task-Specific Agents.’’ HPTSA is a framework that focuses on teams of AI agents collaborating to complete penetration tests. The framework architecture is composed of three major agent types: a hierarchical planning agent, a management agent for the task agents, and task-specific expert agents. The hierarchical planning agent instructs the manager agent based on exploration actions it performs on the target environment. The manager agent determines which task-specific agents to employ and can provide previous information produced by 100894 FIGURE 3. Conceptual AutoPT FSM workflow (illustrative, synthesized from source descriptions) showing state transitions between scanning, reconnaissance, and exploitation phases. agents if further testing needs to be conducted. The taskspecific agents perform specific exploits delineated by six categories: XSS, SQL injection (SQLi), Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF), Server-Side Template Injection (SSTI), the Zed Attack Proxy (ZAP), and a generic agent. The agents are enabled to perform penetration testing activities through access to external tools, documentation (on the order of 5–6 documents per vulnerability category), and a prompt template customized per vulnerability with relevant exploitation information [18]. The agents are orchestrated using LangGraph’s agent graph with LangChain; to reduce token usage, client-side HTML is stripped of extraneous tags before being passed to agents, since HTML often dominates token counts [18]. Figure 4 shows the HPTSA interaction architecture. HPTSA’s toolset includes Playwright for website access, a terminal, tools for file management, and both OWASP ZAP and sqlmap for the agents in those respective categories. In evaluation against a simpler agentic framework that gives GPT-4 external tools and Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVE) descriptions, HPTSA achieved successful exploitation at 1.8 times the success rate of that baseline (with CVE info) given multiple attempts, and up to two times better when the baseline lacked CVE information [18]. GPT-4 outperformed open-source models within HPTSA, which were unable to exploit vulnerabilities in the reported tests. The identified cost of utilizing HPTSA was $4.39 per run for the exploit-capable GPT-4 powered model. HPTSA showcases the ability to integrate information from different VOLUME 14, 2026 R. Addis et al.: LLM-Based Intelligent Agents for Cybersecurity task-specific agents to fulfill the current overall testing objective, and its modularity allows task-specific agents to reduce the need to return to previous steps. Limitations include elevated refusal rates and faulty testing strategies with open-source models, and difficulty for exploitation agents when no obvious attack path is present [18]. The detected issues are grouped into vulnerability classes based on predefined rule sets tied to the programming language, configuration standards, and security checkpoints. The process usually involves several layers of analysis: semantic, data flow, control flow, and structural analysis to identify syntax errors, insecure configurations, and unsafe logic pathways. Once the vulnerability is detected, SAST tools generate reports indicating the location of the issue, making it easier for developers to pinpoint and resolve it, as shown in Figure 5 [90]. Traditional SAST tools offer advantages such as enabling early vulnerability detection, working across the entire codebase without requiring execution, and integrating easily into development workflows so that developers can identify weaknesses even as they write code. However, they also have limitations such as being difficult to configure initially, being language-dependent and prone to false positives. Sometimes projects even require layered SAST tools to achieve sufficient coverage. They also lack the ability to reason contextually on developer intent or logic [90]. FIGURE 4. Conceptual HPTSA interaction architecture (illustrative, synthesized from the source description) showing hierarchical planning agent, management agent, and task-specific expert agents. B. AUTOMATED SOURCE CODE AUDITING (SAST) 1) BACKGROUND OF TRADITIONAL AUTOMATED SOURCE CODE AUDITING When testing an application or program’s source code for weaknesses and vulnerabilities, two complementary approaches are typically employed: dynamic analysis, which observes a program’s behavior during runtime, and static analysis, which inspects the source code before execution. This subsection focuses on the latter. SAST is a cybersecurity method that automates static analysis to detect vulnerabilities early in the Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC) by scanning for insecure patterns and rule violations in the source, byte, or assembly code [1]. Transformer-based and embedding-based methods have been applied to vulnerability discovery in lifted code [88], [89]. SAST analyzes source code for potential vulnerabilities to SQL injections, buffer overflows, cross-site scripting, and others. Since SAST operates prior to compilation and execution, it helps developers address security weaknesses and logic errors in the earliest stages of development, reducing both time and cost associated with post-deployment fixes [90]. SAST workflow begins by parsing the source code into a machine-readable format. The tool applies rule-based pattern matching or regular expressions to detect known coding flaws or deviations from secure code standards. VOLUME 14, 2026 FIGURE 5. Conceptual workflow of Static Application Security Testing (illustrative summary of typical SAST stages, not a benchmark of any specific tool). Popular SAST tools include Fortify, SonarQube, CodeQL, and Semgrep. Each relies heavily on syntactic and rule-based matching, enforcing coding standards and flagging insecure patterns in source code. CodeQL performs semantic queries to detect unsafe data flows and logic flaws, as demonstrated in large-scale studies analyzing hundreds of open-source projects [91]. Similarly, Semgrep and SonarQube apply customizable, rule-driven analysis to ensure code quality and security compliance, with comparative evaluations demonstrating their effectiveness and limitations in static analysis workflows [92]. Traditional SAST methods are rulebased and highly dependent on predefined coding standards, making them strong but rigid. As modern software grows in 100895 R. Addis et al.: LLM-Based Intelligent Agents for Cybersecurity complexity, purely syntactic methods are unable to interpret context or intent within code. As discussed in Section II, LLMs are capable of semantic reasoning, context awareness, and adaptive learning, making them suitable for integration into the SAST workflow. 2) RECENT ADVANCES OF AI AGENTS FOR SAST LSAST introduces a new class of hybrid auditing frameworks that amalgamate traditional SAST techniques with the contextual reasoning capabilities of LLMs. In a typical LSAST architecture, the SAST engine performs an initial scan using established static analysis rules to flag syntactic and structural anomalies; the resulting potential vulnerabilities and code snippets are then passed to an integrated LLM for deeper semantic reasoning and correlation across the codebase [1]. According to Keltek et al. [1], LLMs, while effective at identifying syntactic flaws, are limited by their static training data. To overcome this limitation, the authors propose incorporating Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), which provides dynamic, internal knowledge sources so the model can access up-to-date information such as recent CVEs while avoiding direct, uncontrolled external API calls and mitigating privacy and data-leakage risks [1], [93]. Frameworks such as Secure MultifacetedRAG (SecMulti-RAG) extend this by integrating internal enterprise documents, curated expert content, and in some cases filtered external LLM outputs, using a locally hosted model for most tasks and reserving external LLM access for vetted inputs [93]. Compared to traditional SAST, LSAST systems that integrate RAG can better infer developer intent, interpret code in semantic context, and prioritize findings by exploitability over raw rule-trigger frequency, leading to fewer false positives [1]. LSAST agents extend traditional static analysis along two main dimensions. First, contextual reasoning: through integrated retrieval and reasoning loops, they correlate issues across function boundaries and application-level context, uncovering higher-order vulnerabilities that often evade purely rule-based methods [1], [78]. Second, adaptive learning: whereas traditional tools require manual updates to rule sets when new Common Weakness Enumerations (CWEs) arise, LSAST frameworks use RAG and incremental fine-tuning to incorporate emerging vulnerability knowledge, and dynamic retrieval allows internal knowledge bases to be updated for better recall on zero-day or recently published CWE categories [1], [93]. A notable example is the IRIS framework proposed by Li et al. [78], which integrates LLMs with static analysis to enable whole repository reasoning and uses LLMs to infer taint specifications and analyze context, reducing reliance on manually crafted rules. When evaluated on real-world Java projects, IRIS powered by GPT-4 detected 55 vulnerabilities, significantly more than traditi

