{"id":"R0014","study_type":"Tutorial and survey; step-by-step walkthrough of agentic penetration testing","RQ1 task_stage":"Automated vulnerability discovery and penetration testing; source code auditing, fuzzing, and network security assessment","RQ1 domain":"Web applications and APIs; source code, networks, and infrastructure","RQ1 orientation":"Primarily offensive and evaluative agents for vulnerability discovery","RQ2 architecture":"Surveys single- and multi-agent designs, including Planner–Executor and hierarchical task-specific agents; Observe–Think–Act loops, memory, and tool-use interfaces","RQ2 llm_used":"Surveyed examples include GPT-4, GPT-4o, GPT-4o mini, Claude 3, DeepSeek, and Qwen; no single model used by the survey","RQ2 adaptation":"Surveys prompt engineering, CoT, ReAct, Plan-then-Execute, RAG, instruction-tuning, and incremental fine-tuning","RQ3 autonomy_level":"Surveyed agents autonomously explore targets, select tools, form and verify vulnerability hypotheses, and generate and refine payloads","RQ3 hitl":"Human-defined goals and execution limits; human oversight and gating of risky tools","RQ4 dataset":"155 cited sources spanning 2022–early 2026; no original experimental dataset stated","RQ4 environment":"No original testbed stated; surveyed evaluations include Docker sandboxes, Natas wargame challenges, and HackTheBox UnderPass","RQ4 metrics":"Source-reported task completion, exploitation success rate, testing cost, compromise time, and vulnerabilities detected","RQ4 baseline_type":"Surveyed comparisons include other agentic frameworks, human penetration testers, and traditional SAST","RQ4 headline_result":"Survey retained 155 unique sources; reported AutoPT results include double the simple tasks and nearly 10 times the complex tasks versus ReAct","RQ4 compared_to":"Surveyed comparisons name ReAct, the fastest human, and a GPT-4 agent with external tools and CVE descriptions","RQ4 code_available":"not stated","RQ4 data_available":"not stated","RQ5 risks_addressed":"Hallucination, context limitations, operating costs, unreliable tool calls, privacy and data leakage, dual-use misuse, and indirect prompt injection","RQ5 mitigations":"Safety-scoped sandboxes, strict firewalling, logging, schema validation, risky-tool gating, human oversight, and internal RAG with vetted external inputs","author_limitations (verbatim)":"Adjacent applications that share the same agentic substrate (malware reverse engineering, supply-chain package vetting as briefly discussed in Section III-B, defensive SOC automation, and LLM-based digital forensics) are acknowledged as promising extensions but are not surveyed comprehensively.","author_future_work (verbatim)":"Future directions include advancements in multi-agent collaboration, mechanisms for agent selfimprovement, and improved transparency into agent behavior.","evidence (page/section)":"Abstract, p. 100884: study type, tasks, workflow, risks, future work; I-C–D, pp. 100885–100886: scope, orientation, corpus, limitations; II-A–C, pp. 100886–100892: models, prompting, architecture, memory, tools, oversight, risks; III-A, pp. 100892–100895: environments, metrics, comparisons, AutoPT results; III-B2, p. 100896: RAG, fine-tuning, privacy mitigations. Supplied text ends during III-B2."}
END_JSON
